"1. BMC Surg. 2023 Feb 4;23(1):28. doi: 10.1186/s12893-023-01926-7.\n\nEvaluation of the practice of surgical antibiotic prophylaxis in a Zonal \nReferral Hospital in Mbujimayi, Democratic Republic of the Congo (DRC).\n\nMbuyamba HT(1)(2), Muamba CM(3)(4), Binene SK(3), Uwonda SA(3).\n\nAuthor information:\n(1)Department of Surgery, Universit\u00e9 Officielle de Mbujimayi (UOM), Mbujimayi, \nDemocratic Republic of the Congo. hervembuyamba8@gmail.com.\n(2)Department of Surgery, Muhimbili University of Health and Allied Sciences \n(MUHAS), Dar es Salaam, United Republic of Tanzania. hervembuyamba8@gmail.com.\n(3)Department of Surgery, Universit\u00e9 Officielle de Mbujimayi (UOM), Mbujimayi, \nDemocratic Republic of the Congo.\n(4)Department of Surgery, Bonzola Zonal Referral Hospital, Mbujimayi, Democratic \nRepublic of the Congo.\n\nBACKGROUND: Surgical Antibiotic Prophylaxis (SAP) is helpful in preventing \npatients from developing Surgical Site Infections (SSI). In Mbujimayi, the \ndocumentation on the practice\u00a0of SAP is outdated and inadequate. The last study \nwas conducted more than 5\u00a0years ago. This study aims at assessing the compliance \nof the practice of antibiotic prophylaxis in the surgical and \nobstetrics-gynecology departments of the Bonzola Zonal Referral Hospital (BZRH) \ncompared to the international standards.\nMETHODS: A prospective observational study was conducted from March 2020 to \nMarch 2021 involving 324 surgical patients who received antibiotic prophylaxis. \nInterventions were assessed as \"compliant\" if all the variables individually \ncomplied with the criteria for antibiotic prophylaxis use.\nRESULTS: Three hundred and twenty-four patients were enrolled in this study. \nCompliance was found to be 87.35% for the indication for administration; 0.31% \nfor the choice of the molecule; 3.65% for the time of the first administration; \nnone for the duration of antibiotic prophylaxis. Therefore, the overall \ncompliance was nil. This study shows a significant gap when the current practice \nin Mbujimayi town is compared to the recommendations of international societies.\nCONCLUSION: SAP is often indicated in accordance with international \nrecommendations in Mbujimayi. However, the choice of the molecule, the dosage, \nthe time of first administration and the duration of SAP deviate from them. \nThus, the compliance of SAP is nil.\n\n\u00a9 2023. The Author(s).\n\nDOI: 10.1186/s12893-023-01926-7\nPMCID: PMC9899390\nPMID: 36739370 [Indexed for MEDLINE]\n\nConflict of interest statement: Dr. Herv\u00e9 Tshikomba Mbuyamba reports the support \nfrom the Else-Kroner-Fresenius Stiftung foundation through the BEBUC Scholarship \nProgram. Funds received were used outside of this study. The authors therefore \ndeclare that there is no conflict of interest."